when I first booted up high on life for  the first time I knew what I was getting  myself into I'm familiar with not only  the work of Justin Roiland but also his  game Studio squanch games so I had an  idea of the sort of Comedy that was  coming my way but I did not expect  however was a 3D shooter with  metroidvania Vibes that Echo some of the  best games of my youth and more  importantly does them Justice  [Music]  high on life tells the story of an  unnamed protagonist whom everyone calls  Bounty Hunter even their own sister  fighting against a drug cartel that's  invaded Earth the cartel wishes to round  up every human on the planet and sell  them as the drug which other aliens can  consume via elaborate machines our  Bounty Hunter hero is armed with  galleons a race of talking guns and each  Galleon possesses its own attacks and  abilities the concept is admittedly very  weird but it's a well-told story that  kept me guessing until the end at this  point it Bears acknowledging that this  is 100 a Justin Roiland project complete  with all the Hallmarks of his comedic  philosophy rapid fire monologues fart  jokes demolition of the fourth wall  ad-libs and dark humor it's all  intertwined within the game's narrative  and presentation if things like Rick and  Morty Trover saves the universe or solar  opposites aren't your cup of tea then  this won't be either that said I have a  very high tolerance for this sort of  goofiness and I found myself laughing  throughout the 10 hour Adventure I got a  kick out of the fact that squanch games  licensed four full-length b-movies for  players to enjoy simply because it could  every time a voice actor in clear ad-lib  mode would laugh in the middle of a  diatribe I would also laugh the  referential comedy also got a reaction  out of me particularly mentions of other  video games  okay you know what it looks like some  simple platforming you know like but  you'd see in like Lucky's tail on uh you  know or um you know that one uh that one  game that we that we all know and love  Mario Land or whatever  there's a genuine charm in all of it  sure some of the jokes don't land in the  rambling of the gun in your hand can  sometimes go on too long but it's clear  in every attempt that the development  team was having fun making high on life  I never knew what was gonna happen next  as the scene progressed and that lack of  predictability enhanced The Experience  honestly this unruly approach is the  only way certain jokes can work which  includes for example an entire scene  that plays out in space Applebee's  however squanch games also knows this  brand of humor isn't for everyone and it  provides the option to tone down banter  so those turned off by consonant chatter  can still enjoy the game I left my  settings to frequent banter and didn't  think it was too too much in comparison  I didn't find the banter to be as  egregious as what earned Horizon  forbidden West criticism earlier this  year this could be because the  characters weren't speaking aloud about  puzzles in front of me which made the  dialogue feel more natural for the  setting but once you get past the  goofiness and focus on the game itself  high on life delivers a solid 3D shooter  experience that emulates the exploits of  Samus Iran each of the different biomes  I explored were brimming with secrets  from living chests filled with gold to  random NPCs offering a quick Zinger or a  brief side quest as the game progresses  vertical movement is introduced thanks  to finding a jet pack and that opens up  exploration even more these worlds are  large as well which the game tries to  counter with a waypoint system that  allows you to see which direction your  objective lies and how far it sits  sometimes this system gets its wires  crossed though and reaching one-way  points somehow turned into me returning  to the Waypoint I just came from but  more often than not a simple tap of the  d-pad pointed me back in the right  direction the waypoints also become  somewhat of a crutch in the later parts  of the game as not using them sometimes  led me to traveling to the wrong part of  the map and getting lost it's not a  perfect system but the waypoints are  helpful more often than not the galleons  each stand out from one another both in  their quips and how they're used in  combat Kenny is the resonant pistol Gus  is the shotgun knifey is the uh knife  and Sweezy acts as the Needler from Halo  the most interesting of the weapons is  creature which acts as a sort of Pikmin  device launching small creatures at  enemies for damage over time all four  are potent in combat as they possess  advantages over certain foes and make it  essential to switch through guns during  a fight each gun has a second power that  aids with traveling and environmental  puzzles which slowly opened more of the  world to me as I progressed Kenny issues  blast of thick slime which will hit  designated obstacles and allow you to  pass Sweezy can fire a shot that slows  time in the area where it lands making  her the perfect choice to get past fast  spinning fans that would otherwise cause  damage these special shots can be used  in fights as well Kenny's slime shots  will launch enemies into the air for  extra hits for example which gave me  even more options using the gatlions in  battle is a lot of fun and the enemies  fit right into the weird and silly  aesthetic of the rest of the game the  bad guys are each covered in a sort of  yellow goo the origin of which I won't  spoil and as you deal damage the goo  wears off exposing their gray bodies  underneath while strange it serves as an  easy visual aid to how much damage  you've done to a certain enemy and it  also allows you to create your own weak  points so say an enemy is hiding behind  cover and there's a patch of gray skin  Exposed on its arm targeting that spot  will make the enemy fall more quickly  it's an ingenious idea of showing combat  damage on enemies without giving them  health bars or something similar that  said most of the enemies I encountered  are dumb as rocks I could run up to any  of the normal Grunts and either shoot or  melee them to death without taking a lot  of damage but there were times where I  was overwhelmed and had to retry a  battle but those were more due to me  being overzealous than enemies  outsmarting me boss fights aren't much  better as the majority of them boil down  to Simply shooting and dodging some of  them shake things up a bit whether they  Implement a special shot of The Gatlin  you're about to rescue or they give you  multiple bosses to fight at once but at  their core it's more of the same combat  you get from the grunch just with bigger  baddies while overall combat is fun  where it feels tedious is the length of  some of the skirmishes each encounter  tends to play out in waves with one or  two sets of enemies coming out before  the battle is over some of them stretch  far longer than that to the point where  Kenny the pistol says here we go again  and I'm feeling exactly the same these  fights become a test of patience as well  as a test of skill boss fights don't  have this issue for the most part but  some of them fall into their own issues  one fight in particular still makes me  angry just thinking about how difficult  it became in its final stage ultimately  high on life is in its own weird way a  take on what a modern Metroid Prime game  could be through the lens of Justin  roiling's comedy Antics there's a  similar sense of exploration mixed with  fast-paced moments of combat only here  it's also swelling with expletive Laden  jokes and sometimes incoherent rambling  the story offbeat as it may be is told  incredibly well with characters and  moments I'll be referencing for a while  even if you're not a fan of the kind of  humor that high on life presents the  game that's here is worth the trip  hey uh my name is Thomas schnitzel and I  just want to say I didn't kidnap that  alien baby the trial wasn't publicized  it wasn't on air like nobody saw it I I  was I was pronounced Innocent but nobody  really saw it so I I'm I'm buying these  commercials to just make sure everyone  knows I did not kidnap the alien baby I  did not do it
 

